  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>15</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. The exponent of 2 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. The exponent of 3 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. The exponent of 5 is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> The whole expression is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mn>3</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mn>5</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. A rational product of distinct prime powers with integer exponents is an integer exactly when every remaining exponent is nonnegative: different primes cannot cancel one another.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> Since a, b and c are nonzero integers, the conditions are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Answer A. Zero exponents would be allowed mathematically, but a, b and c are excluded from zero here.</p>"
